---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import TrustStrip from '../../components/TrustStrip.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CTABand from '../../components/CTABand.astro';
import FAQAccordion from '../../components/FAQAccordion.astro';

const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "How do I know if my EV circuit has a wiring problem?",
    "answer": "Signs include breakers that trip after 1-3 hours of charging, reduced charging speeds reported by the vehicle, warmth or discoloration at the panel or outlet, and burning smells near the charger or panel."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can you repair wiring without replacing the whole circuit?",
    "answer": "Often yes. If damage is localized to terminal connections, a single junction box, or a specific breaker, targeted repair is faster and less expensive than full circuit replacement."
  },
  {
    "question": "What wire size is correct for EV charging?",
    "answer": "For 32A continuous: 8 AWG minimum. For 40A continuous: 8 AWG (short runs) or 6 AWG. For 48A continuous: 6 AWG (short runs) or 4 AWG (runs over 50 feet). We calculate based on actual circuit distance."
  },
  {
    "question": "Why does my breaker trip but the charger shows no error?",
    "answer": "The breaker may be failing thermally due to internal wear, bus bar oxidation, or overcrowding in the panel. We test the breaker independently to determine if the breaker itself needs replacement."
  },
  {
    "question": "Is circuit repair covered by homeowner insurance?",
    "answer": "It depends on the cause. Damage from covered events like power surges may be claimable. We provide detailed diagnostic reports that support insurance claims when applicable."
  }
];
---

<Layout
  title="EV Charger Circuit Repair & Installation in Issaquah | Wiring Experts"
  description="EV charger circuit repair and dedicated 240V circuit installation in Issaquah WA. Fix tripping breakers, burnt wires, and damaged conduit. Call (425) 380-0019."
  canonicalPath="/services/ev-charger-circuit-repair-installation/"
>
  <Breadcrumbs items={[
    { name: 'Home', url: '/' },
    { name: 'Services', url: '/services/' },
    { name: 'EV Charger Circuit Repair & Installation' }
  ]} />

  <section class="hero-section">
    <div class="container">
      <div style="max-width: 840px;">
        <span class="section-tag" style="background: rgba(132, 204, 22, 0.2); color: #84cc16;">Specialized EV Service</span>
        <h1>EV Charger Circuit Repair & Dedicated Circuit Installation in Issaquah</h1>
        <p class="hero-lead">Behind every charging failure is a circuit problem: undersized wire, a loose terminal, a cracked conduit, or a worn breaker. We repair damaged EV charging circuits and install new dedicated lines built to handle continuous high-amperage loads.</p>
        <div class="hero-cta-wrap">
          <a href="tel:+14253800019" class="btn btn-lime"><span>📞</span><span>Call (425) 380-0019</span></a>
          <a href="/locations/" class="btn btn-outline-white">View All 15 Service Areas</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <TrustStrip />

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="rich-content" style="max-width: 900px; margin-inline: auto;">
        <h2>What This Service Covers</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Repair of damaged 240V branch circuit conductors and conduit</li>
          <li>Replacement of tripping or overheating dual-pole circuit breakers</li>
          <li>Re-termination of loose, oxidized, or burnt wire connections</li>
          <li>Installation of new dedicated EV charging circuits</li>
          <li>Conductor upsizing for circuits experiencing voltage drop</li>
          <li>GFCI breaker replacement and configuration</li>
        </ul>

        <figure class="seo-service-figure" style="margin-block: 2.25rem; text-align: center;">
          <img 
            src="/images/panel-upgrade-200-amp-circuit-breakers.jpg" 
            alt="Repairing damaged 240V EV branch circuit wiring and breaker in Issaquah WA garage"
            width="900"
            height="500"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
            style="width: 100%; height: auto; max-height: 440px; object-fit: cover; border-radius: 12px; border: 1px solid #cbd5e1; box-shadow: 0 10px 25px -5px rgba(0,0,0,0.08);"
          />
          <figcaption style="margin-top: 0.65rem; font-size: 0.88rem; color: #64748b; font-style: italic;">
            Replacing damaged conductors, fixing junction boxes, and correcting undersized wire runs.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>


        <h2>Technical Background & How It Works</h2>
        <p>The dedicated circuit between your electrical panel and your EV charger carries sustained high current for hours every night. Over time, thermal cycling causes copper conductors to expand and contract at terminal connections. If screws loosen even slightly, resistance increases and generates dangerous heat.</p>

        <figure class="seo-service-figure" style="margin-block: 2.25rem; text-align: center;">
          <img 
            src="/images/ev-charger-troubleshooting-diagnostic-multimeter.jpg" 
            alt="Certified electrician testing voltage stability and impedance on dedicated EV circuit in Issaquah 98027"
            width="900"
            height="500"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
            style="width: 100%; height: auto; max-height: 440px; object-fit: cover; border-radius: 12px; border: 1px solid #cbd5e1; box-shadow: 0 10px 25px -5px rgba(0,0,0,0.08);"
          />
          <figcaption style="margin-top: 0.65rem; font-size: 0.88rem; color: #64748b; font-style: italic;">
            Upgrading faulty circuit breakers and fixing dual-GFCI nuisance trip conflicts on dedicated lines.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>


        <p>Additionally, circuits installed with undersized wire (such as 10 AWG instead of 6 AWG for a 48A load) experience excessive voltage drop over distance, causing the vehicle to reduce charging speed or halt entirely. We verify wire gauge, circuit length, and terminal integrity on every repair call.</p>

        <p>This service is available throughout all 15 Issaquah neighborhoods, including <a href="/locations/issaquah-highlands/">Issaquah Highlands</a>, <a href="/locations/talus/">Talus</a>, <a href="/locations/klahanie/">Klahanie</a>, and <a href="/locations/olde-town-issaquah/">Olde Town Issaquah</a>. Browse our full <a href="/locations/">locations hub</a> to find your area.</p>

        <h2>When to Contact Us</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Your EV circuit breaker trips repeatedly</li>
          <li>You see discoloration or melting at wire connections</li>
          <li>Your charger reports low voltage or reduced charging speed</li>
          <li>Conduit is physically damaged from garage activity</li>
          <li>You need a new dedicated 240V circuit for an EV charger</li>
        </ul>

        <figure class="seo-service-figure" style="margin-block: 2.25rem; text-align: center;">
          <img 
            src="/images/level-2-ev-charger-installation-issaquah.jpg" 
            alt="New dedicated 50-amp 240V breaker installed and torqued to manufacturer specifications"
            width="900"
            height="500"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
            style="width: 100%; height: auto; max-height: 440px; object-fit: cover; border-radius: 12px; border: 1px solid #cbd5e1; box-shadow: 0 10px 25px -5px rgba(0,0,0,0.08);"
          />
          <figcaption style="margin-top: 0.65rem; font-size: 0.88rem; color: #64748b; font-style: italic;">
            Precision torque screwdriver calibration prevents loose connections and resistance heating.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>


        <p>For technical questions about ev charger circuit repair & installation in Issaquah, call our dispatch line directly at <a href="tel:+14253800019">(425) 380-0019</a>. Licensed under <a href="https://apps.leg.wa.gov/WAC/default.aspx?cite=296-46B" target="_blank" rel="noopener">WAC 296-46B</a> and fully compliant with NEC Article 625 standards.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
